{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested: exact head ffa498e2b2b02e6b0991fe4caa2fcbe165ad5254 on planner/publisher-member-20261113, its one artifact `notes/2026-11-13-publisher-member.md`, for request 35cbcb29 under promise 885d9876 and review request e9a1813e. The route is sound, and every form that slice P1 needs exists at main 57e893c0. Two statements need correcting before Hugh ratifies the note. One is about revocation, and one is about a guard.\n\nRequired change 1: revoking a publisher does not stop its Git writes.\n\n- Section 6, row \"The publisher's key is lost or misused\", and section 7, row \"How does a room stop a bad writer?\", say that membership revokes the key and staff appoint another publisher. That stops the old publisher's `published` acts. It does not stop its pushes. The old publisher still holds the Git credential, and no act of the record can revoke a credential at the Git host.\n- After revocation the old publisher can still move the branch. The new publisher then meets the row \"Another writer moved the branch\" and stops, so the record holds, but the published pages can show content that no entry covers until staff repair the branch.\n- State the rule: revoking a publisher has two parts. Membership revokes its key, and whoever issued the Git credential revokes it at the host. The record does the first part and cannot do the second. Name who holds that duty when the operator runs the publisher and when the room's owner does, which is the first of Hugh's two decisions.\n\nRequired change 2: the guard \"`through` is an entry of this scope\" needs a second guard.\n\n- The declared `fact` guard (`packages/derive/src/program/guard-value.ts:121-133`) checks the entry's kind and that its definition name equals `under`. A fetched fact of another scope that runs a definition with the same name also passes (`:129-130`).\n- P1 must add `equals { a: { field: \"through\", part: \"scope\" }, b: { scope: true } }`. Both operands exist: `part: \"scope\"` reads a local entry's scope as this scope (`read-value.ts:27`, `operand.ts:566`), and `{ scope: true }` passes validation (`validate/operands.ts:194`). Name that guard in section 4.2, so that P1 builds it and witness 5 or a new witness covers a foreign `through`.\n- A foreign `through` would set the cursor to an entry of another room. A reader's check of section 7 would then fold the wrong journal.\n\nThe kernel question. I confirm the reading of section 8. Each form of section 4 exists at 57e893c0:\n- Slots: a party slot with `list: false` (`validate/items.ts:42`), a record value slot (`definition.ts:80`, as in `platform/src/destination.ts:128`), a `commit` slot that is not required (`definition.ts:79`, as in `destination.ts:214`), and a reference slot of field type `fact` (`definition.ts:92`).\n- Guards: `{ signer: [\"publisher\"] }` (`validate/guards.ts:170-178`, `guard-value.ts:108`), `equals { field: \"base\", slot: \"head\" }` (an absent field and an empty slot both read `null`, so both-absent passes: `read-value.ts:47`, `operand.ts:397-400`, `664-667`), and `{ unset: \"repository\" }` (`guard-value.ts:111`).\n- Effects: set a party, a value and a reference slot (`validate/effects.ts:121-195`).\n- Roles: `declareRoles` only checks spelling, and `agent` is a fixed role (`packages/platform/src/membership.ts:84`).\n\nP1 changes `examples/lending` alone, adds nothing to `packages/contract`, `derive` or `bytes`, and names no K item. Moving the platform's own publication changes promise 11 and needs section 7 of `docs/kernel.md`, as section 8 says and slice P4 carries.\n\nWhat I read: the whole note, and the source behind sections 1.1, 4, 5 and 8. Section 1.1's count holds: `file-publication` reserves 18 operations (`destination.ts:2820-2822`).\n\nWhat I checked: main 57e893c0 is an ancestor, the head changes one path, and the artifact covers it at the head. The artifact is neither stale nor retired. `check-notes` reports 0 warnings, the commit-message check passes (3 checked, 0 flagged), and `git diff --check` is clean. I ran no tests, since the head changes no code.\n\nArchitecture: sound. A conditional ref update and a readable branch let the record hold only signed results and no `unknown`. The fold has fixed inputs, author and time, so two publishers make one commit and a reader can check each trailer with no platform code. The open questions of section 10 sit in a later note, and P1 does not depend on them.\n\nSecurity: required changes 1 and 2. Otherwise the trust moves as section 7 says: a signed act replaces an unsigned `outcome`, and the record names the answerable member. A publisher can still sign a false report, as section 7 states, and a reader's fold detects it.\n\nSimplification: no required change. Three facts for P1:\n1. `repository` must be declared not required, because `bind-repository` sets it after the genesis.\n2. The role table also appears in `examples/lending/dap.application.json:228-230`, beside `roles.ts`. An installed room learns `lending.publish` through an ordinary `set-actions` act.\n3. The field type of `through` must list each kind it can name, at most 64 (`validate/fields.ts:74-80`).\n\nThis verdict authorizes no landing. Hugh ratifies the note.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:4948ac8636cea6d078094cc8e0e1ef85ff35b54f","binding":"assigned","head":"ffa498e2b2b02e6b0991fe4caa2fcbe165ad5254","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:cc74dc43c47845445d7a08a90b3b34ec6fd1366d\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:885d9876ee81ce9ccee711063914f38e3a4c04bc\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:72b1d654fe0afd073e82f1a83c5aa22945a69633","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}